
export anchor AcceptedDraftedStoriesGoIntoTheBacklog2:
    userPrompt:
        \\\
        When a drafted proposal for a new story is accepted, the new story goes into the Backlog, at its end, instead of into Next sprint. This covers Accept on a card and "Accept all" in the [Drafting](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#drafting) panel, and proposals from Ask answers too, since they are accepted the same way. Update [Creating](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#creating) so accepted proposals are named as the exception to "however a story is added, it goes into Next sprint". Update the Accept paragraph of [Proposals](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#proposals) so a new story goes into the Backlog. The story is still in its mode, still marked "From agent" with its sparkle, and keeps its spec citations. The card's note now reads "Added to Backlog", and its link opens the story there. Accepting a proposal never adds anything to Next sprint, so it never starts a sprint under [Auto Start](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#auto-start). An accepted story reaches a sprint only when it is moved to Next sprint, as the [Sprint](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#sprint) section says. Leave these alone: "Add to" proposals, which still add a step to the named story; the ribbon's and Planning's "New story", which still add to Next sprint; Duplicate; and the Ask card's "Add to sprint" in [Narrow](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/ask-conversation/index.md#narrow), which still adds to Next sprint.
        \\\

    mode: code

    newConversation: false

    systemPrompt:
        \\\
        We're working on the code, located in ./src. Change the code so it does what the prompt asks, as the spec describes it.

        When the work is about Suspense, its modes, its prompts, or its questions, or you are to write a prompt or a question for the user, first read .suspense/suspense-fluency.md once in this conversation, unless you already have: it is how Suspense works.

        The spec's Piton source is in ./spec. Don't change it, and don't read it to learn what the spec says: read the compiled reference instead, as above. If the prompt asks for something the spec doesn't describe, or describes differently, say so in your reply rather than working around it.

        Once you have read what the change depends on, and before your first change, write the constraints this task must meet to ${UNDERSTANDING_FILE}, once. Rewrite it only when a constraint in it turns out to be wrong, never after each page you read. It is a Markdown list and nothing else: one line per constraint, at most twelve, each a single short, specific statement of what must be true, written as a link to the compiled reference file it comes from, such as `- [The chain tab has no bottom border in combined mode](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/chat-input/index.md#chain)`. Only list what the spec says; leave out anything you are deciding yourself. Replace a constraint when you learn it was wrong rather than adding another.

        End with a reply of a few sentences saying what changed, or for a question, the answer, not a recap of the work or of what you read.

        This prompt is the second step of a chain. Its first step changed the spec to meet it, and the spec has been built since. Now change the code so it does what the spec now says, without changing the spec. Follow the spec the prompt references, and the anchors the spec step names below. Where the spec and the prompt disagree, follow the spec, and say so in your reply.

        The code is located in ./src.

        The spec's source, which this step doesn't change, is in ./spec.

        The prompt the chain was sent:

        ${SPEC_PROMPT}

        What the spec step said it changed, its final output:

        ${SPEC_RESULT}
        \\\

    codeTask:
        prompt:
            \\\
            When a drafted proposal for a new story is accepted, the new story goes into the Backlog, at its end, instead of into Next sprint. This covers Accept on a card and "Accept all" in the [Drafting](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#drafting) panel, and proposals from Ask answers too, since they are accepted the same way. Update [Creating](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#creating) so accepted proposals are named as the exception to "however a story is added, it goes into Next sprint". Update the Accept paragraph of [Proposals](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#proposals) so a new story goes into the Backlog. The story is still in its mode, still marked "From agent" with its sparkle, and keeps its spec citations. The card's note now reads "Added to Backlog", and its link opens the story there. Accepting a proposal never adds anything to Next sprint, so it never starts a sprint under [Auto Start](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#auto-start). An accepted story reaches a sprint only when it is moved to Next sprint, as the [Sprint](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#sprint) section says. Leave these alone: "Add to" proposals, which still add a step to the named story; the ribbon's and Planning's "New story", which still add to Next sprint; Duplicate; and the Ask card's "Add to sprint" in [Narrow](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/ask-conversation/index.md#narrow), which still adds to Next sprint.
            \\\
        result:
            \\\
            Accepted new-story proposals now go to the end of the Backlog instead of Next sprint. `piton check` reports no problems and `piton build` wrote 86 files.

            - **Creating** (`.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#creating`): accepted proposals are now named as the one exception to "every added story goes into Next sprint", whether they come from drafting, research or an Ask answer's proposal card.
            - **Accepting** (`#proposal-cards`):
              - **Where it goes:** a new story goes to the end of the Backlog, whether you press Accept on its card or "Accept all".
              - **What it keeps:** its mode, its "From agent" sparkle and its spec citations.
              - **No sprint starts:** accepting never adds anything to Next sprint, so it can't trigger the automatic start. The story reaches a sprint only once you move it to Next sprint.
              - **The card's note:** it now reads "Added to Backlog", and its link opens the story where it is.
            - **Unchanged:**
              - "Add to" proposals still become a new step of their story.
              - "New story" in Planning and the ribbon, and Duplicate, still add to Next sprint.
              - The Ask card's "Add to sprint" still adds to Next sprint.

            The section I edited is the Proposal Cards section, `#proposal-cards`, since that's where the Accept paragraph lives.

            **Anchors the code should follow:** `Planning` `#creating`, `#proposal-cards`, `#auto-start` and `#sprint`, and `AskConversation` `#narrow`, which is unchanged.
            \\\

    sentFrom: AcceptedDraftedStoriesGoIntoTheBacklog

    model: opus
